  "notes": [
   "Parts are unchanged from step 1: 12 parts (breadboard, Nano 33 IoT, IR receiver, IR LED, one 330 ohm resistor, seven jumper wires). All are catalogue types, so no new part types.",
   "The catalogue breadboard has only 30 columns, but the Fritzing image is numbered 30 to 63. I compressed the layout to columns 1-27 and kept the same topology. The lower block of the image (rows a-e) is the 't' bank. The Nano straddles the gap with its bottom pin row in 't' row e and its top pin row in 'b' row f.",
   "Nano pin order was inferred, not printed in the image. Bottom row, left to right: D1, D0, RST, GND1, D2 to D12. Top row, left to right: VIN, GND, RST, VUSB, A7 to A0, AREF, 3V3, D13. The nano pin 'GND1' is used for ground.",
   "Image wiring, read as nets: the IR receiver leg at the left (red wire) goes to the + rail, the middle leg (black wire) to the - rail, and the right leg through the long green wire to D11. D11 is the default RECV_PIN of the IRrecvDumpV2 sketch. A red wire from the Nano's 3V3 pin feeds the + rail, so the receiver runs at 3.3 V, which suits the Nano's 3.3 V logic.",
   "Receiver pin order: the catalogue lists GND, VCC, DAT, but the image wires the left leg to the + rail and the middle leg to the - rail. I followed the image, so VCC is in col 1, GND in col 2 and DAT in col 4.",
   "IR LED: the cathode (left leg) goes by a black wire to the - rail. The anode (right leg) goes through the 330 ohm resistor and then a blue wire to D9. The resistor is on the anode side. D9 is inferred from the position in the image.",
   "In the image the black wire at the Nano's lower row lands near the RST/GND area. I connected it to the GND1 pin, since it is the ground return to the - rail.",
   "Only the Nano pins that carry a connection (D9, D11, GND1, 3V3) are listed as joined to the breadboard. The remaining header pins sit in unused strips and connect to nothing.",
   "The sketch, Arduino Cloud Thing and Alexa skill are software and not part of the graph."
  ]
